@node Vision and needs
@section Vision and needs

Tharsis will let a player run the original @emph{Quake} campaign from legally
supplied data on a current desktop, while preserving game logic and making the
renderer a practical demonstration of current Rust GPU technology. The player
gets a classic mode that retains the original visual rules and an enhanced mode
that adds resolution, filtering, lighting, and presentation improvements without
changing simulation state.

Players need predictable controls, working saves and demos, clear errors for
missing or damaged data, and mods that cannot compromise memory safety. The
maintainer needs a real game workload with which to evaluate safe Rust, Rust
shaders, portable GPU compute, and reproducible rendering. Every fidelity or
performance claim therefore needs a repeatable test, a named input, and a
recorded environment.

The product is a port, not a remake. Original behaviour remains authoritative
when an enhancement would alter weapon timing, physics, monster decisions,
trigger order, network messages, or demo playback.

@node Goals
@section Goals

@multitable @columnfractions .12 .88
@headitem ID
@tab Goal
@item G1
@tab Complete every map in the selected free and user-supplied @file{id1} campaign data from a new game to the finale.
@item G2
@tab Reproduce the reference server state for the differential corpus, reporting the first divergent frame and field on failure.
@item G3
@tab Forbid @code{unsafe} in simulation, QuakeC, protocol, and asset-format crates.
@item G4
@tab Reject malformed game data and QuakeC operations with bounded diagnostics rather than a panic, hang, or out-of-bounds access.
@item G5
@tab Build GPU shaders from Rust source and test their shared shading functions on both CPU and GPU.
@item G6
@tab Render static world visibility and draw submission through GPU compute and indirect draws on capable adapters, with a tested baseline path.
@item G7
@tab Meet the declared frame-time, memory, startup, and soak-test budgets on recorded reference systems.
@item G8
@tab Build ordinary engine packages on pinned stable Rust, without requiring a nightly compiler on a player's machine.
@end multitable

@node Non-goals
@section Non-goals

@itemize @bullet
@item
QuakeWorld, Quake II, Quake III, Hexen II, and the 2021 Kex engine.
@item
CSQC, DarkPlaces-only features, general FTE compatibility, VR, mobile, and
console releases.
@item
Redistribution of registered, shareware, mission-pack, or re-release game data
unless its specific licence grants that right.
@item
A new game design, rebalanced weapons, replacement campaign, or mandatory
high-resolution assets.
@item
Public Internet multiplayer, a master server, matchmaking, accounts,
anti-cheat, telemetry, advertisements, or automatic mod downloads in the MVP.
@item
A hard dependency on experimental mesh shaders, ray tracing, a specific GPU
vendor, or a nightly host toolchain.
@item
Map editors, model editors, and a general-purpose game engine.
@end itemize

