@node Users and stories
@section Users and stories

@table @strong
@item Campaign player
Starts a new game with data discovered from a documented location, chooses a
renderer, completes the campaign, saves and loads progress, and plays the
original demos.
@item Mod player
Selects a game directory, keeps original and mod files separate, and receives a
useful error when content is unsupported or malformed.
@item Performance-focused player
Uses uncapped rendering and interpolation while the server preserves its
original timing rules, and inspects CPU and GPU timing through a debug overlay.
@item Engine maintainer
Runs headless differential tests, shader checks, fuzzing, and timedemos from a
script without registering game assets in the public repository.
@item Packager
Builds from committed source and generated shader artifacts using a pinned
stable host toolchain, with no asset download in the default build.
@end table

@node Game data
@section Game data

The MVP supports the original BSP29, MDL, SPR, WAD2, LMP, WAV, QuakeC
@file{progs.dat} version 6, protocol 15 demo, text save, palette, colormap, and
configuration formats. Game-directory layering follows vanilla search-order
rules. Mission packs, BSP2, protocol 666, coloured @file{.lit} lighting, and
re-release extensions are outside the MVP.

Public CI uses synthetic fixtures whose source and licence are committed. It
may fetch shareware or freely licensed replacement data only from a pinned URI
with a pinned SHA-256 and a recorded redistribution licence. Registered data is
never committed, uploaded as a build artifact, or required for a public CI job.
Local release validation covers registered @file{id1} data supplied by the
maintainer.

The original GPL release explicitly says that game data remains under its
original terms. The build therefore treats code availability and asset
availability as separate questions; an asset being downloadable does not by
itself prove that Tharsis may redistribute it.

@node Platforms
@section Platforms

The MVP targets 64-bit Linux on Wayland and X11. Windows and macOS builds are
engineering previews until their input, audio, filesystem, rendering, and
packaging acceptance tests pass. No 32-bit target is planned.

The portable GPU path uses wgpu on Vulkan, Metal, or DX12. The GL backend is a
compatibility option only after its feature floor passes the same golden-scene
suite. A system without a usable GPU path receives a clear diagnostic and may
use the software reference renderer; it never encounters an unexplained panic
or blank window.

@node Compatibility
@section Compatibility

The behavioural oracle is the id Software GPL WinQuake / GLQuake source release
built out of tree with floating-point contraction disabled. Original QuakeC 1.06
and protocol 15 are the initial compatibility targets. QuakeSpasm-family
behaviour is evidence for later extensions, not permission to change vanilla
semantics silently.

A documented vanilla defect is preserved when gameplay, demos, or mods depend
on it. A defect that only permits memory corruption, arbitrary file access, or
an unbounded computation is replaced with a checked error and recorded as an
intentional safety difference.

@node Functional requirements
@chapter Functional requirements

@menu
* Gameplay::               Server, QuakeC, physics, saves, and demos.
* Rendering::              Classic and enhanced visual behaviour.
* Audio and input::        Sound, music, and controls.
* User interface::         Menus, console, errors, and accessibility choices.
