@end menu

@node Gameplay
@section Gameplay

@itemize @bullet
@item
The engine runs an authoritative local server and a client joined through the
same message boundary used by NetQuake, including during single-player games.
@item
The QuakeC VM executes every version 6 opcode and vanilla builtin required by
the selected data, with checked global, field, string, function, and entity
access.
@item
Physics preserves hull tracing, entity-link order, movement modes, pushers,
water, collision response, player thinking, and trigger activation order.
@item
The host preserves vanilla time-based behaviour. A separate deterministic
fixed-step mode exists for tests and never becomes an implicit gameplay change.
@item
New game, map transition, intermission, episode progression, finale, death,
restart, and difficulty selection work without a renderer or audio device.
@item
Protocol 15 demos record and play back. Save files round-trip all vanilla state
and reject damaged or unsupported data without replacing an existing save.
@item
The @option{-game} directory layers content over @file{id1} using vanilla
precedence, and the active search path is visible in a diagnostic command.
@end itemize

@node Rendering
@section Rendering

Two presets share one renderer and one simulation.

@table @strong
@item Classic
Original palette and colormap rules, light styles, dynamic-light falloff,
turbulent surfaces, sky, sprites, particles, view blends, nearest filtering,
and an optional low internal resolution. Golden scenes compare this preset with
the software reference renderer.
@item Enhanced
Arbitrary output resolution, linear filtering, anisotropy where available,
MSAA, interpolated model animation, full-precision lighting, and HDR output on
capable surfaces. Enhanced presentation never exposes entities that the
original visibility rules would hide.
@end table

The renderer uploads static world geometry and lightmap data once per map,
evaluates changing light styles and dynamic lights on the GPU, and uses GPU
visibility and indirect draws on the GPU-driven tier. Alias models, sprites,
beams, particles, water, sky, view models, and the HUD are rendered by the same
frame pipeline.

Adapter loss triggers a bounded renderer rebuild from retained CPU-side
resources. A failed rebuild switches to the software renderer or returns to a
recoverable menu with a plain-language error; it does not terminate the server
or destroy the active save.

@node Audio and input
@section Audio and input

Sound effects preserve vanilla channel allocation, looping, attenuation,
ambient sounds, static sounds, and stereo spatialisation. External music follows
the documented @file{music/trackNN} convention using a pure-Rust decoder selected
at design time. Missing audio hardware selects silent mode with one warning;
gameplay continues.

Keyboard and mouse input supports remapping through vanilla configuration
commands. Menus are keyboard-complete. The default bindings do not capture
Insert, Caps Lock, or platform assistive-technology chords. Optional gamepad
support does not delay keyboard and mouse support.

Although the registered game carve-out applies, Tharsis adopts reduced-motion
options for view bob, damage tilt, and screen flashes, independent volume
controls, remappable controls, and text alternatives for plot-critical spoken
content when such content is present in supported data.

@node User interface
@section User interface

The game provides single-player, load, save, options, controls, video, and quit
menus; a drop-down console with history and completion; the original HUD,
centre prints, intermission, and finale screens; and a no-data screen that
explains where to place legally obtained files.

Configuration remains compatible with vanilla @file{.cfg} scripts. Unknown
commands and cvars produce bounded warnings rather than execution through a
system shell. Screenshots, saves, demos, traces, and configuration use documented
per-user directories and never overwrite the input PAK files.

The no-data and diagnostic interfaces use the Steelbore theme and bundled OFL
fonts. In-game text uses the supplied game's font data.

@node Developer tools
@section Developer tools

A non-interactive @command{tharsis-tools} command provides PAK, BSP, model,
progs, demo, save, shader, and trace inspection. Machine-readable output has a
versioned schema, stable exit codes, and no ANSI decoration when piped.

Headless modes run server traces, software-rendered golden frames, GPU golden
frames, and timedemos. Trace comparison reports the first divergent frame,
entity, and field. Performance output identifies build commit, content hash,
adapter, driver, backend, enabled features, resolution, and present mode.

@node GPU technology
@chapter GPU technology

The technology baseline is pinned deliberately rather than inferred from a
@code{latest} dependency range. Versions below were checked on 2026-10-27 and
are re-qualified before implementation and at each release gate.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .24 .58
@headitem Technology
@tab Baseline
@tab Purpose and decision
@item wgpu
@tab 30.0.1
@tab Safe native Rust graphics and compute over Vulkan, Metal, DX12, and GL; the only GPU API in the MVP renderer.
@item Naga / WGSL
@tab Naga 30 family
@tab Shader validation and translation for portable backend output. WGSL is the compatibility output, not a second gameplay shader implementation.
@item Rust GPU
@tab 0.11.0
@tab Rust shader crates compiled to SPIR-V; current release adds Naga 30 support and uses @code{nightly-2026-08-15}.
@item cargo-gpu
@tab Pinned to Rust GPU
@tab Manages the shader-only nightly toolchain and builds shader artifacts without making the host engine nightly-only.
@item winit
@tab Pin at G2
@tab Cross-platform window and input ownership on the main thread.
@item Software renderer
@tab Ported reference
@tab Pixel oracle and no-GPU fallback; not a second product with different visual rules.
@end multitable

@menu
* Shader strategy::        One Rust shading source and reproducible outputs.
* Capability tiers::       Baseline, GPU-driven, and experimental paths.
* Modern renderer::        How current GPU features benefit Quake.
* Rejected defaults::      Why not a larger or lower-level stack.
@end menu

@node Shader strategy
@section Shader strategy

A @code{no_std} shading crate contains palette, light-style, dynamic-light,
water, sky, fog, and tone-mapping functions. CPU tests and the software renderer
call the same functions as the GPU entry points. Rust GPU compiles the entry
points to SPIR-V; Naga validates and translates the result to WGSL where needed.
CPU/GPU layout types have explicit alignment and size tests.

Release source contains generated SPIR-V and WGSL artifacts with licence
coverage. Ordinary builds consume those artifacts on stable Rust. A dedicated CI
job regenerates them with the pinned Rust GPU toolchain and fails on unexplained
output differences. Toolchain upgrades are separate reviewed changes, never a
side effect of a feature commit.

If Rust GPU cannot produce valid, performant output for a required backend, the
project records the evidence and uses reviewed WGSL behind the same shader
interface. This fallback protects the release; it does not silently fork visual
behaviour.

@node Capability tiers
@section Capability tiers

@table @strong
@item Tier 0 --- software
Runs the ported software renderer. Used for oracle tests and systems without a
suitable adapter.
@item Tier 1 --- portable GPU
Uses core wgpu rasterisation, texture arrays or atlases, depth testing, MSAA
where selected, and conventional draw submission. This tier implements every
MVP visual feature except GPU-driven culling.
@item Tier 2 --- GPU-driven
Adds compute PVS/frustum culling, indirect draw generation, optional multi-draw,
optional binding arrays, GPU timestamps, and HDR output after querying adapter
and surface capabilities. Every feature has a Tier 1 equivalent.
@item Tier 3 --- experimental
Contains mesh-shader and ray-query experiments. These wgpu APIs remain
experimental, are disabled by default, and are not part of the MVP or its
performance claims.
@end table

Device creation requests only capabilities used by the selected tier. Startup
logs the adapter, backend, limits, selected tier, and every rejected optional
feature. A shader or pipeline failure at an optional tier falls back one tier;
a failure in the portable path is surfaced rather than hidden.

@node Modern renderer
@section Modern renderer

The renderer uses current technology where it removes measured work from Quake's
original bottlenecks:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Compute visibility.} The CPU supplies a compact PVS representation and
camera data. A compute pass tests surface bounds and emits draw commands,
removing the per-frame CPU surface loop on Tier 2.
@item
@strong{Indirect and multi-draw submission.} Static geometry remains in large
GPU buffers. The CPU submits a small fixed set of passes instead of one draw per
visible surface. A fixed-count path is used when indirect-count support is
absent.
@item
@strong{Resource arrays with fallback.} Binding arrays reduce bind-group churn
on capable adapters. Atlases and array textures preserve the same shader inputs
on the portable tier.
@item
@strong{GPU light evaluation.} Light styles are small per-frame data and dynamic
lights are a storage buffer. The CPU does not rebuild lightmaps for flicker,
muzzle flashes, rockets, or explosions.
@item
@strong{HDR presentation.} wgpu 30 surface colour-space and display-HDR
capabilities enable HDR only when both are present. SDR remains a tested output
with explicit tone mapping and gamma behaviour.
@item
@strong{Timestamp observability.} Supported timestamp queries feed per-pass
measurements. Unsupported adapters use CPU frame measurements without pretending
that they are GPU times.
@item
@strong{Render bundles and pipeline reuse.} Static HUD and invariant pass work
is reused where measurements show a benefit. Pipeline caches are optional and
validated against adapter and driver identity.
@end itemize

Mesh shaders are evaluated later for meshlet world and model rendering because
current wgpu supports them across major native backends, but their experimental
API status keeps them out of the release path. Ray queries are similarly limited
to an opt-in research branch for shadows and ambient occlusion; the MVP never
requires ray tracing or claims it is stable.

@node Rejected defaults
@section Rejected defaults

@table @strong
@item Bevy
Not selected as the engine framework. Tharsis needs vanilla Quake state,
ordering, timing, and data layouts more than a general ECS editor stack. Small
purpose-built crates keep those contracts visible.
@item ash
Not the portable baseline. Direct Vulkan would add backend-specific safety and
compatibility work before product fidelity is established. A direct-backend
spike requires a measured missing capability, not a preference for lower-level
code.
@item OpenGL-only renderer
Rejected because it makes compute, indirect submission, HDR, and modern shader
workflows either unavailable or inconsistent across target systems.
@item Nightly host compiler
Rejected for normal builds. Rust GPU's compiler is isolated to shader artifact
generation, while the engine and packages remain on pinned stable Rust.
@end table

