@node Crate boundaries
@section Crate boundaries

@multitable @columnfractions .28 .72
@headitem Crate
@tab Responsibility
@item @code{tharsis-math}
@tab C-faithful scalar, vector, angle, and evaluation-order helpers.
@item @code{tharsis-fs}
@tab PAK access, search paths, game layering, and confined writes.
@item @code{tharsis-data}
@tab Bounded asset parsers and validated decoded types.
@item @code{tharsis-qc}
@tab QuakeC bytecode, VM, builtin dispatch, and run-error boundary.
@item @code{tharsis-sv}
@tab Authoritative server, physics, entity state, and saves.
@item @code{tharsis-proto}
@tab Protocol 15 messages, demo codec, and loopback transport.
@item @code{tharsis-cl}
@tab Client messages, interpolation, temporary entities, and snapshots.
@item @code{tharsis-shade}
@tab Dependency-light @code{no_std} functions shared by CPU and GPU.
@item @code{tharsis-shaders}
@tab Rust GPU entry points; no game rules.
@item @code{tharsis-soft}
@tab Software reference renderer and headless framebuffer output.
@item @code{tharsis-gpu}
@tab wgpu resources, passes, capabilities, and recovery.
@item @code{tharsis-app}
@tab winit lifecycle, audio, menus, console, and command line.
@item @code{tharsis-tools}
@tab Headless inspection, conversion, comparison, and benchmarks.
@end multitable

The simulation, VM, data, and protocol crates forbid @code{unsafe}. Renderers
never mutate server or client state. Filesystem code never depends on a renderer.
Only @code{tharsis-gpu} depends on wgpu, and only the application crate depends
on the windowing and audio backends. Public crate interfaces do not expose
third-party lock or channel types.

@node Concurrency
@section Concurrency

The authoritative server is serial. QuakeC calls, entity links, trigger order,
and floating-point evaluation order are observable game behaviour; splitting
them would add scheduling variance for little measured gain. This is a deliberate
Priority 1 decision, not an omission of concurrency.

The main thread owns the window and client event loop. A bounded SPSC loopback
connects server and client. Immutable render snapshots allow presentation to
overlap the next server frame. No renderer callback writes into the snapshot or
server arena.

Stateless loading, mip generation, fixture processing, and corpus comparison use
a bounded worker pool. Each job owns its output and publishes it through one
completion channel. Parallel command encoding is adopted only if a benchmark on
the reference scene beats one encoder without increasing frame-time variance.
Audio receives bounded commands on its backend thread; an overrun drops a
defined class of commands and increments a counter rather than blocking the
simulation.

@node Renderer data
@section Renderer data

A render snapshot contains read-only visibility, entity, light, camera, and HUD
state for one presentation instant. Double buffering gives the renderer a stable
snapshot while the client builds the next one. Frame-local uniform and storage
writes use a bounded ring allocator with explicit fence lifetimes.

Static map resources survive until map unload or renderer recreation. The CPU
retains enough validated source data to rebuild GPU resources after device loss.
No per-frame path allocates an unbounded vector, waits on a worker queue, or
reads back the indirect buffer to decide what to draw.

@node Failure handling
@section Failure handling

Parsers return typed errors with a bounded path or byte-offset diagnostic. Every
length, count, offset, string, recursion depth, and allocation is checked before
use. QuakeC has a configurable runaway-instruction limit and a recoverable
run-error path. Fatal errors return to a menu or headless exit code; malformed
external input never calls @code{unwrap}, @code{expect}, or a panic-based
indexing path.

Degraded behaviour is explicit: no audio means silence, no optional GPU feature
means a lower tier, no GPU means software rendering, and no game data means the
no-data screen. A corrupt save never destroys the previous file; writes use a
temporary file, flush, and atomic rename within the same user data directory.

@node Security and legal
@chapter Security and legal

@menu
* Threat model::           Mods, files, and process boundaries.
* Network boundary::       What the MVP does not expose.
* Licensing and assets::   GPL obligations and data separation.
* Privacy::                Local-only behaviour.
@end menu

@node Threat model
@section Threat model

PAKs, maps, models, sounds, demos, saves, configuration, and QuakeC bytecode are
untrusted. The VM cannot invoke arbitrary process commands or write outside its
permitted game-data area. Console commands originating from content pass through
the same allowlist as user scripts; no content string becomes a shell command.

Path handling rejects traversal, absolute paths supplied by content, symlink
escape from the active write root, and unbounded path lengths. User-selected
read-only base directories are allowed; engine-created writes are limited to the
documented per-user directory. Release builds retain bounds checks and shader
runtime checks.

@node Network boundary
@section Network boundary

The MVP contains no Internet or LAN listener and performs no automatic network
request. Local server/client messages use the in-process loopback only. Adding
UDP networking is a later product change that re-enters design review for threat
model, licence, assurance category, rate limits, sandboxing, and protocol fuzzing.

Original NetQuake provides no modern authentication or encryption. A future
secure transport will be a separate versioned protocol and will not misrepresent
protocol 15 as protected. Post-quantum migration is considered only when such a
transport is designed; it is not retrofitted into the offline MVP.

@node Licensing and assets
@section Licensing and assets

The id Software release contains GPL version 2 text and source headers granting
@emph{either version 2 or, at your option, any later version}. Before derived
code lands, each incorporated file's notice is checked and recorded. The new
combined engine uses GPL-3.0-or-later where that grant applies. Upstream notices,
licence texts, and credits are preserved; no inherited file is silently
relicensed.

The main game is a local desktop application. A future separately distributed
network daemon is reviewed for AGPL-3.0-or-later under the Standard. Software,
documents, generated shader artifacts, fixtures, and third-party content each
carry REUSE-compatible provenance.

Original game assets are inputs, not build outputs or bundled dependencies.
Free fixtures and replacement data have their own licence record and checksum.
The project name and presentation do not imply id Software endorsement.

@node Privacy
@section Privacy

Tharsis has no analytics, crash upload, advertising, account, tracking ID, or
third-party web resource. Logs and traces stay local unless the user explicitly
sends them elsewhere. Data discovery checks documented local paths and does not
scan unrelated directories. Build-time asset fetching is opt-in for test data,
pinned, and never part of an ordinary player build.

@node Verification
@chapter Verification

Category D requires only a smoke test, but fidelity and unsafe input are central
to this product, so the project voluntarily adopts differential testing,
parser fuzzing, GPU validation, and measured performance gates.

@menu
* Fidelity evidence::      Oracle comparisons and shader tests.
* Performance budgets::    Reference scenes and measurable targets.
* Acceptance::             The release-level definition of done.
@end menu

